TWO LIVE DEFECTS FOUND BY GATES, BOTH FIXED. (1) The render's mix bus counted MIXERS, and F1 had quietly made those different from CHANNELS — one mic cut into three spans opened three mixers and exported at 1/3 gain, 9.5 dB down, with the loudness normalizer spending its makeup budget undoing it. Mixers for different spans never overlap in output time, so they cannot sum and must not be staged against each other; busGainFor counts distinct channels. (2) analyzeSilence carried its own copy of the mixer construction, and F5b had just made the two capable of disagreeing about how long a span lasts — an envelope measured on a different timeline proposes cuts in the wrong places. One construction now, in audio.ts, used by both.

F2B. A zoom is not one keyframe, it is a move — anchor and target ZOOM_MOVE_MS apart — so dragging either marker drags the group and keeps its duration; leaving the anchor behind would stretch the ease, which is a different edit from the one being asked for. Measured on the prod build: the pair sits at x=515 and 647, dragging the target 120 px lands them at 635.5 and 767.5 — both moved, gap unchanged. The RIG was wrong before the product was, again: it dispatched pointermove on the window, and startDrag listens on the element via pointer capture, which a synthetic pointer id never gets.

P1, CODE HALF. The problem was shape, not coverage: capabilities.ts asked what ENGINE this is, and the answer depends on the OS too. Chromium on WINDOWS carries the machine's audio on a monitor share; the same Chromium on macOS and Linux only ever carries a tab's or window's, so calling both "Tab Audio" is wrong on one. And Gecko is a case that reads like a bug report — Firefox ACCEPTS `audio: true` on getDisplayMedia and returns video only, no error and no track, so offering the channel there records a silent take and says nothing. It is dropped, with copy that says what Firefox DOES rather than "not available in this browser", which is true and useless. displayAudioScopeFor is pure and the PROBE VETOES THE TABLE. aacEncode is advisory only, because codecs.ts probing the encoder is a better authority than a table. Proven end-to-end on the production build under a spoofed Firefox 131 UA: engine reads gecko, channels come back Screen/Camera/Mic, all eight QA gates pass. TWO GATES LEFT AND BOTH NEED DOWNLOADS PO MUST APPROVE: Playwright's gecko driver for the real Firefox run (this runner speaks CDP, which Firefox does not), and a Windows machine for the one row that cannot be spoofed — navigator.userAgentData beats the UA string, which is correct behaviour, so a Windows UA on a Mac still reports macOS.

