td 2026-08-24 (4) — PO "ROADMAP ALL O AND F IN ONE SESSION": O5 DONE, AND ITS CENTRAL PREMISE WAS FALSE. The task said "the render is main-thread and strictly serial — decode awaits draw awaits encode", and pipelining it was supposed to buy multiples. render.ts now counts every stage, and on a 12 s 1080p edited take the main-thread lane reads decode-wait 1295 ms · draw 450 ms · ENCODE-WAIT 28 ms · finalize 65 ms, against 962 ms to decode the same frames with no draw and no encode at all. Two things follow and both are load-bearing: the encode was never the stall, because mediabunny's VideoEncoderWrapper already keeps four frames in the VideoEncoder queue and returns before they are encoded; and the decode cannot hide behind the draw, because the expensive part of "decode" is JS on the same thread. An encode lookahead window AND a decoder prefetch pump were both built, measured, and DELETED for changing nothing — which is the honest outcome of (b) and is recorded as note 13 so the idea is not proposed a third time. (a) THE WORKER SHIPPED AND IS THE DEFAULT. Getting the render off the main thread meant porting the certified audio path off Web Audio, which is [Exposed=Window]: AudioBufferSink → AudioSampleSink, AudioBufferSource → AudioSampleSource, one shared makeStereoSample builder so the instant path and the render cannot drift. Same PCM, different carrier — and the fidelity oracle is what proves it: THD −77.4 dB, IMD −57.6, separation 53.4 dB, limiterHits 0. Measured warmed and in BOTH orders (A,B,B,A): 60 s take worker 6.00× realtime vs main 5.93× (the ≥5× gate MET, engines at parity), 12 s take worker 5.29× vs 5.56× as fixed worker costs amortize, and ALL FOUR LANES PRODUCE BYTE-IDENTICAL FILES. The first version of that rig ran worker-first once and reported the worker 35 % slower — note 10's fifth instance, a cold-start artifact. HONEST ON THE GATE: "zero main-thread long tasks >50 ms" cannot fail and therefore cannot pass — both engines report zero, because the in-thread render awaits a decoded sample every frame and an await ends the task. Scheduling lateness replaced it: worker 31-48 ms vs main 77-104 ms at 12 s, but only 184 vs 217 ms at 60 s, and this rig's main thread is otherwise idle so it understates the case that matters. (c) SMART CUT SHIPPED, MERGED, DEFAULT OFF behind `?smartcut=1`. A trim-only edit changes no pixel, so the composite already holds what the output wants: copy its packets and re-encode only [cut → next keyframe]. Both halves share ONE avcC, so the boundary encoder's emitted decoder description is compared BYTE FOR BYTE with the composite's before a packet is written and a mismatch throws to the render — strictness we can afford because the capture encoder is ours. 30 s take, trim-only, both boundaries deliberately off the 2 s keyframe grid, five runs: 669-944 ms against 3718-3780 ms for the render (4.3-5.6×, the ≤1 s gate MET), 90-94 % of frames copied, picture matching the render at 37.5-42.8 dB. The file sits ONE FRAME ahead of the render — and the rig's control shows the SHIPPED INSTANT PATH sitting one frame ahead of the same render too (41.8 and 38.4 dB at +1, no smart cut in that comparison at all), so the offset is the composite's and smart cut adds nothing. IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT FOR A GATE REASON, NOT AN EVIDENCE REASON: nothing in CI exports a CUT take through this path, because the oracle's trim goes through exportRecording directly and would pass while never touching it. Same call as O4's dormant flip, same precedent; flipping it is now a light task on the map. (d) THE CODEC LADDER SEAM SHIPPED WITH THE DEFAULT UNCHANGED: av1-hw → hevc-hw → avc → avc-opus → vp9-webm, rungs above the avc floor unreachable unless a caller passes allowAboveFloor, and a rung above the floor must additionally probe as HARDWARE (a software hevc encode is slower than the avc it replaces). This makes O11d's standing decision executable rather than restating it — the file is blind-shared and no probe can ask the recipient what it plays. (e) VFR not attempted. ALSO CLOSED: O8's export peak-memory band — the oracle now fails above 12 MB of output held at once and prints `peakOut=`, reading 0.1 MB on the 6 s take. O1 measured that claim in 2026-08-23 and nothing had enforced it since, and the failure it guards is invisible on a short take, which is exactly why the band belongs on one. ITS FIRST RUN READ `n/a` AND THAT WAS REAL: the export had just moved into a worker, so getLastScratchStats() was main-thread-blind — the new gate caught its own prerequisite, and the worker now forwards both RenderStats and ScratchStats. SCOPE, STATED PLAINLY: PO asked for all of O and F in one session and that was not achievable — O5 alone consumed it, because disproving its premise was the work. O3b, O4-polish, O6, O9, O10, F6, F7c and F8 are untouched and each still costs a session; the READY map says so. Gates: typecheck · 287 tests · build (first paint 234.7 kB against the ≤300 kB O7 gate; the export worker is its own 540 kB chunk) · oracle PASS on the default v2 engine (sync 33.9-62.4 ms, tail 68-72 ms, export 5.06-5.21×, peakOut 0.1 MB) · oracle --engine=v1 PASS (sync 60.4, tail 72 ms, export 5.14×) · oracle:fidelity PASS.

td 2026-08-24 (5) — PO "GO O5-FLIP": THE GATE GOT BUILT AND THEN REFUSED THE FLIP, WHICH IS THE POINT OF BUILDING IT. The task was to route the oracle's non-frame-aligned trim through the editor's export decision so smart cut ran under the sync band, then turn it on. Step one exposed why nobody had done it: the oracle's rig never recorded a COMPOSITE, so `recording.composite` was always absent, so neither packet-copying path could run there at all — routing the trim through the ladder without fixing that would have fallen straight through to the render and passed while proving nothing. SHIPPED, and all of it stays regardless of the flip: compose/choose.ts, the instant → smartcut → render ladder as ONE function returning which path ran and why each faster one declined (it used to live inside EditorScreen, where no gate could reach it — that is the root reason the oracle had only ever measured the render); RecordOptions.composite, so the rig records a live composite exactly as production does with the same v2-or-v1 capability choice; the trimmed export driven through the ladder with its OWN sync gate on the same ≤90 ms band; an anti-vacuity verdict that FAILS the run when a fast path silently declined, because a gate measuring the wrong file is worse than no gate; `npm run oracle -- --no-composite` as the A/B lever; and `inst=`, `trim=<path>/<sync>`, `compT0=` printed on every oracle line. THE RIG WAS CHECKED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED, note 10 discipline: 3 runs each, composite OFF 50.2/67.8/62.4 ms vs ON 55.1/37.9/55.7 ms — no systematic shift, and an early single 87.1 ms reading was a loaded-machine outlier rather than a signal. THEN IT FAILED THE FLIP: with smart cut on, the trimmed export read sync mean 150.3 ms against the 90 ms band in the one run where flash/click pairs could be matched at all, and could not be matched in the other two. IT IS NOT SMART CUT'S BUG, and the same run says so — the SHIPPED INSTANT PATH, measured on the same take by the same metric, reads 97.4 and 102.4 ms on v2 against that take's render at 52.5 and 64.3, and 244.8 ms on v1. Every unedited export takes that path; it is what PO uses. So the flip is REVERTED and the finding is promoted to P0-instant-sync at the top of the READY map. MEASURED BUT NOT ROOT-CAUSED, stated as such: the composite's first video packet sits at 133-300 ms on v2 while CompositeRecording carries no offset field, so both copy paths assume composite time IS recording time — but v1 reads compT0=0 and is the WORSE of the two, so the clock origin is a contributing quantity and not the whole explanation. The next session decides what a composite timestamp MEANS and gives the type a way to say it. ONE HONEST CALL RECORDED RATHER THAN BURIED: the instant path's sync is now measured on every run but NOT banded, because banding it today turns CI red on a defect that predates this session and blocks every other task; it is printed instead so it cannot regress unnoticed, and it gets its band in the session that fixes it. ALSO SURFACED: with a composite running alongside, the audio boundary jump reads 0.002-0.064 where it used to read a flat 0 (band 0.1) — production takes have always carried that load and the oracle simply never did. Gates: typecheck · 287 tests · build (first paint 234.7 kB) · oracle --cold=3 PASS 3/3 with the flag off (sync 37.9/52.5/64.3, tail 76/76/84 ms, export 4.46-5.24×) · oracle --engine=v1 PASS · oracle:fidelity PASS.
