po+td 2026-08-24 (same day, superseding the zip shape above): AI EXPORT IS ONE FILE, AND THE FILE IS A PDF. PO: "it must be one file that any ai will understand, not human watchable" — and every richer layer is only a roadmap CANDIDATE until PO tests the first real file. Format reasoning, recorded so it is not relitigated: PDF is the only one-file format every major AI (Claude chat/Code/API, ChatGPT, Gemini, API agents) ingests natively as text+images together; a zip needs a shell and a filesystem, markdown cannot carry images in one file, base64-images-in-text arrive as text and explode the token bill, and mp4 is the thing agents cannot afford to watch. Consequences for AI1: index page first (pure selectable text — take facts, event lines, keyframe list with page numbers so a paging agent descends selectively), then one pixel-delta keyframe per page (JPEG/DCTDecode, ≤1024 px, timestamp caption as text, full-res crop on-page only when the changed region is small — signal-derived), page dimensions equal the image so no pixel is padding, NO video track inside the file (the ordinary MP4 stays a separate export), and a zero-dependency minimal PDF writer of our own. AI2-AI7 marked CANDIDATE and frozen until PO's first-file report exists.
po+td 2026-08-24 (same day, AI export design): THE CURSOR IS CLASSIFIED, NOT CAPTURED AROUND. PO raised that the cursor moves every frame yet is rarely useful — it is the one thing that would defeat "near zero keyframes when static". Decision: one frame-diff, classified by size and persistence — tiny+moving+transient = cursor (never a keyframe; becomes a low-rate pointer trail in the index text, v1's only interaction signal while DOM events wait on AI2); tiny+stationary+blinking = caret (ignored); small-but-persistent = real content like tooltips/menus (keyframe, crop on it); large = content/burst (keyframes as normal). A change at the cursor's last rest position captions "change at cursor" — click inference with zero new capture surface. The FILTER ships unconditionally (it is a threshold); the TRAIL ships only if the rig proves the pixel-cursor detector reliable, and is dropped rather than shipped degraded. Hard gate added to AI1: cursor-only wandering over a static screen → ≤1 keyframe.
td 2026-08-24 (2): "FIX THIS UNTIL IT IS GONE" — DEVICE RELEASE INVERTED, THE FOURTH AND LAST TIME. PO, with a screenshot of Chrome's share indicator: the wait after the picker closes is too long although the screen is already shared; pressing record again to stop leaves the indicator lit through a refresh; a later refresh trades it for a mic indicator and the next take sits on "waiting for mic". Happens often, not always. Three previous fixes (ledger 2026-08-23 entries 4, 5, 6) each released devices from somewhere DOWNSTREAM of acquisition — the channel list, then `acquiredStreams`, then the picker's undelivered tracks — so every new path was a new chance to miss one, and missing one is not cosmetic: the browser keeps the claim, so the NEXT getUserMedia blocks and the app wears the "waiting for mic" label for someone else's bug. NEW OWNERSHIP: `deviceGuard.ts` registers every stream at the getUserMedia/getDisplayMedia call itself (three sites in acquire.ts, before any await) and `releaseAllDevices()` can always find it. It installs its own pagehide net at the FIRST device, which closes the window the PO was actually refreshing in — the session's own unload guard was only installed once arm() RETURNED, i.e. never in a wedged start. FOUR ORDERING DEFECTS, all "a release sitting behind something with no deadline": (1) doCancel released media only AFTER `await Promise.all(stopped)` with no timeout — a recorder that never fires onstop or an AudioWorklet that never delivers a first sample kept every device live AND stuck the button on "Cancelling…", since createCaptureSession awaited cancel before rethrowing; cancel now releases synchronously as its first act and every join below it is bounded, and createCaptureSession no longer awaits the promise (the guarantee is in the synchronous prefix). (2) doStop released after `closeWriter`, whose write chain has no deadline — a slow disk kept the camera on after the user had pressed stop and moved on; release now sits between the composite's (bounded) stop and the writers, so the instant export still keeps its audio tail. (3) The prewarmed AudioContext was closed only on discardRuntime, so every cancelled arm leaked one. (4) An armed session that never reached the store — anything throwing between createCaptureSession returning and setSession — was unreachable and unstoppable; CaptureScreen now cancels it, and treats a press landing in that gap as the cancel it was meant to be instead of swallowing it. ALSO: a granted mic/camera was timed against the 120 s HUMAN budget whenever there was no screen picker to overlap (the grant probe was skipped entirely), so an audio-only take with a wedged-but-granted mic froze for two minutes instead of eight seconds — the probe now runs in both paths. And the `slow=` harness finally emits arming progress, so "Waiting for microphone…" is reproducible without hardware. VERIFIED IN A REAL BROWSER, not argued from the code (the first time in this bug's history): the preview launcher's EPERM: uv_cwd is the agent spawner having no TCC access to ~/Downloads, worked around by `scripts/mirror-for-preview.sh` + the `inout-tmp` launch config. Driving production code with canvas/oscillator tracks as stand-in devices: cancel mid-arm ends the display track and leaves 0 guarded streams; a mic answering 1.1 s after the cancel is turned straight off; a modelled single-claim mic that blocks the next getUserMedia while a track is live no longer blocks it — take 2 arms in 2.0 s with screen+mic and nothing missing; pagehide during arming ends the screen track; a full take still records both channels and still produces a composite. Gates: 324 tests (10 new), typecheck clean for every file this session touched. STILL OPEN, PO's call: synchronized start (2026-07-20) means the take waits for the slowest device even with the screen already shared — the wait is now bounded and its cause removed, but 8 s of "Waiting for microphone…" over a live screen is the design, not a bug.

