td 2026-08-24 (3): THE WAIT AFTER THE PICKER WAS OUR IPC, NOT THE HARDWARE. PO on the start-policy question: "it must start together immediately, why the fuck waiting is happening, thats what you need to fix" — synchronized start (2026-07-20) STANDS, the wait does not. Measured first, in a real browser driving production code with canvas/oscillator stand-in tracks: arm() itself costs 22 ms, so every second the PO waits after the picker closes is a device opening, and the mic is supposed to have opened DURING the picker. It wasn't. The concurrent-with-picker dispatch — the entire instant-start win — was gated on `await isGranted(...)`, i.e. `navigator.permissions.query`, an IPC to the browser process that is at that moment putting a modal screen picker on the screen. Whenever that answer came back after the picker closed, getUserMedia was dispatched only then and the mic's whole hardware spin-up ran on the user's clock with the screen already shared. Nothing in the code said so and no timeout fired: it just silently degraded to serial. FIX: grants.ts caches what the browser last told us (localStorage + memory, primed at MOUNT by warmCapturePipeline — a permission lookup, not an acquisition, so the no-idle-device rule is untouched), so a known-granted mic/camera is dispatched in the SAME TICK as getDisplayMedia with nothing awaited in front of it. The live probe still runs and still picks the budget (the raw promise is already in flight when it answers), so a permission revoked since the last take still gets the full human budget for its prompt — the cache decides WHEN to ask, never what to believe. An unknown device keeps the old order on purpose: a first-time user must not get a mic bubble stacked under the picker. A grant is also learned from any successful acquisition, so the next take is concurrent even if query() lied. MEASURED, same rig, 2 s picker + 1.5 s mic spin-up + a query the picker holds for 2 s: dispatch-behind-the-probe = mic start +2994 ms, wait after