td 2026-08-24 (10): AI1 REWORKED THE SAME DAY IT SHIPPED, on PO's first real take — a 97 s walkthrough of a product UI, given to an agent TO RECREATE THE UI AND ITS ANIMATIONS. Two verdicts, both right, both calibration rather than design. FIRST, "ai didnt understood its video": the AI opened the PDF and ASKED WHAT TO DO WITH IT, because page 1 opened with machine facts (`clock: recording epoch`, `keyframes are pixel-delta selected`) — a config dump, not a document. The index now briefs its reader before stating anything: what the file is, that the pages after it are frames of ONE recording in time order, why the spacing is uneven, and what to do if it arrived with no instructions; the same line is in /Title, /Subject and every page caption, so a page read alone still says what it belongs to. That is not the no-AI rule being bent — it describes the DOCUMENT, never the recording. SECOND, "it losses way too much frames", and the diff against the MP4 proved it: page 7 was t=12.25 s with the field empty and page 8 was t=17.75 s on a different tab, so the typing, the button turning active, the click and the tab transition were all inside one 5.5 s gap. THREE CAUSES, each fixed and each re-measured on that same recording. (a) A typed word is ~15 grid cells against a content threshold of 36 — and that threshold was only that high to keep a moving cursor out. The pointer is now MASKED OUT of the content metric (cells around where it is and where it was at the reference frame), so the threshold could drop to 17 cells with cursor immunity intact: 1 page out of 96 samples of a wandering cursor. Structural separation beat a threshold doing two jobs. (b) An animation became one page because the pace allowed one per 1.6 s; motion now SUSPENDS the pace, bounded twice — a burst needs TWO consecutive moving samples (one change is an event, e.g. a tooltip, which keeps its close-up) and it is capped at 1.2 s (a scroll is not a transition). (c) The analysis looked 4 times a second; now