td 2026-08-24 (11): THE PDF PAGE LIMIT WAS A REMEMBERED NUMBER, AND IT WAS WRONG — PO caught it with "if pdf is limited than its fucked up format", which was the right challenge to the whole format choice. AI1's budget had been capped at 96 frames on the belief that the readers reject a PDF past 100 pages. CHECKED, not recalled: Claude accepts 32 MB per request and 600 PAGES on 1M-context models — the 100-page figure applies only to 200k-context models — and Gemini accepts 50 MB and 1000 pages, charging a flat 258 tokens per page whatever the page holds. So the format was never the constraint anybody thought it was, and PDF stands: it is still the only single file every major AI ingests natively as text and images. What IS binding is tokens, at ~800 per full-view page, which turns the budget from a hard ceiling into a spend decision: frames now scale with the take's own length (2.5 per second, floor 96, ceiling 300 ≈ 236k tokens ≈ 11 MB), with a hard stop at 28 MB so a pathological recording degrades by stopping rather than by being rejected. On PO's take the budget stopped binding at all — the content earned 165 frames of an allowed 243, and the result is 167 pages, median gap 0.4 s, worst 2.9 s, covered to 97.1 s of 97.3, ~144k tokens, 6.5 MB, built at 0.74× the cost of the same take's full render. THE LESSON IS NOTE 10 IN A NEW COSTUME: the rig is wrong before the product is, and this time the "rig" was a platform limit I recalled instead of verifying — a whole density design was built around a number that was off by 6×. Verify a platform limit before designing to it. WHAT THIS LEAVES OPEN, stated because the next session will want it: 144k tokens is fine on a 1M-context model and tight on a 200k one, and the fix is NOT fewer moments but cheaper pages — a contact sheet of 4-6 small frames per page for motion runs costs ~200 tokens per frame instead of ~800. Not built; it is the obvious next lever now that pages are cheap and tokens are not.
