td 2026-08-25 — "GO ROADMAP, ALL O AND F IN ONE": P0-INSTANT-SYNC FIXED AND GATED, AND IT WAS A MISSING SENTENCE IN THE CONTRACT, NOT A BUG IN A CODE PATH. The composite is a SECOND file with its OWN clock, and that clock does not start when the take does: v1's begins when its MediaRecorder begins — after the <video> elements, the canvas, the audio graph and the durable write stream exist (measured 134-215 ms into the take) — and v2's begins at whichever of audio or video first reached the worker (66-90 ms). CompositeRecording had no field that could say so, so instant.ts and smartCut.ts both assumed composite time IS recording time and placed copied video against audio mixed from the raw channels on the recording timeline. DECIDED: composite timestamps mean what ChannelRecording.startOffsetMs already means for a channel — CompositeRecording.startOffsetMs, produced by BOTH engines, rebased with the channels at stop, ABSENT = 0 = the old assumption kept for takes recorded before the field existed (nothing can recover their origin, and a guess is worse than the behaviour they were exported with). The conversion lives in one pure, unit-tested file (compose/compositeTime.ts) rather than being re-derived per call site. Consumers: instant.ts places every copied packet at its true instant and mixes audio over the whole take; smartCut.ts maps each kept span through the offset, where NEGATIVE composite time means the span begins before the composite's first frame — a real hole in it, not a licence to slide its first frame backwards. MEASURED on the symmetric flash+click metric: v2 instant 185.1 ms → 48.6/59.6/75.8 against those takes' render at 50.0/64.2/54.8; v1 instant 244.8 → 55.7/57.8 against 61.7/59.5 — i.e. the packet copy now sits inside the render's own run-to-run spread, which is the only target that means anything, because it is meant to BE the same file. THE BAND IS ON: oracle-gate now fails a run whose instant path is outside ≤90 ms symmetric, and fails one where