td 2026-08-25 — O4-POLISH: THE RECORDING PREVIEW IS THE COMPOSITOR'S OUTPUT. v2 already composites every frame in a worker from VideoFrames that never touch a <video>; the preview then decoded both sources AGAIN on the main thread and rebuilt the composition in CSS. The worker now takes a canvas (transferControlToOffscreen) and blits the composite into it right after each encode — same task as the draw, which the GL backend requires since it does not preserve its drawing buffer, and counted as its own stat so it cannot inflate paintMs/frameMs/encodeMs. DECIDED: the <video> preview is what runs until the compositor PROVES it can paint — the worker holds its reply until the first blit lands, so the swap cannot flash blank — and a degraded compositor emits composite-preview:false so the UI switches back; v1 keeps the source preview outright, because it composites on the main thread from the very elements the UI is showing and has nothing to hand over. MEASURED rather than asserted (o4step2 gained a preview lane, off by default like the raw lane): 1080p 10 s takes, both orders, blit 0.024/0.034 ms per frame against a ~1.0 ms paint, 0 drops, 12-14 ms encoder latency, and the preview-on runs read ~2 fps HIGHER (27.0/27.2 vs 24.9/25.2) — unexplained and NOT claimed as a win; what is measured is that the preview costs no throughput. Verified on the deployed build with ?synthetic=1: "[capture] recording preview is the compositor's own output", canvas painting, zero <video> in the stage.

td 2026-08-25 — F7c ATTEMPT 5: THE EXPORT PANEL MEASURES ITS SIZE NUMBERS, AND THE ±20 % GATE IS MET. The four earlier attempts all failed on a quantity nobody had counted, so this one counted first: four consecutive GOPs of one encode price a LATER GOP at 1.81x the FIRST on motion content and 0.88x on screen. Through a 12 s file that is (1 + 1.4x1.81)/2.4 = 1.47 against the 1.44x uniform shortfall attempt 4 could not explain — the whole discrepancy, and not a fitted constant. DECIDED: the probe reads TWO CONSECUTIVE GOPS in one forward pass (300 frames, forced keys at 0 and 150 — not a simulation of a file's first two GOPs but literally one) and the estimate blends first-GOP and later-GOP prices by the take's own length, counting keyframes rather than dividing by the cadence (a 6 s take at a 5 s cadence holds TWO keyframes, not 1.2, and on screen content a keyframe is ~200 KB against a ~5 KB delta). Scored on 24 s takes, both contents, twice: worst −13.5 %, most inside ±7 %, against −71 to −84 % for the model it replaces. Two real exports of the deployed build: 540p 279 KB against a shown ~315 KB (+12.9 %), 1440p 320 KB against ~350 KB (+9.4 %). THREE INSTRUMENT FAULTS PAID FOR ON THE WAY, all of which invalidate earlier attempts' numbers: the probe's window ran off the end of a 6 s take so 40 % of every delta mean was one frozen instant repeated; the o11 rig recorded its composite with v1 while production had flipped to v2, so it had stopped scoring the estimator users get; and the first shipping shape re-encoded ONE window twice to obtain a second GOP, which seeks a forward-only channel reader backwards and returns the same frame 150 times (it read 84 % under and looked like a modelling failure). SHIPPING SHAPE: the probe moved to src/core/compose/sizeProbe.ts with FLAT memory — the spike held 150 ImageBitmaps, about 1.2 GB of texture, and now holds one frame at a time, feeding every step's encoder as it composes — takes its encoder options from the render's own 