td 2026-08-25 — F7d OPENED: THE SHIPPED ESTIMATOR'S ANCHOR MOVED UNDER IT AND NOBODY NOTICED. F7's size model is anchored on the COMPOSITE's own byte rate, and O4's default flip changed the encoder that produces the composite. Scored against a v2 composite on 24 s takes it reads −71 to −84 % on motion content and +104 % on screen; on a real 16 s synthetic take the panel opened showing ~4.1 MB for a 540p file that came back at 279 KB. F7c's probe covers the panel now, but that model is still the FALLBACK for every path where the probe cannot run, so it is a task and not a footnote: re-anchor it on the v2 composite's achieved bitrate, or make the fallback say plainly that it is a rough guide. The general lesson is the one already in the notes: a number derived from another component's output has to be re-measured when that component is replaced, and the rig that watches it has to be replaced too — this rig was still recording v1 composites a day after production stopped making them.

td 2026-08-25 — F8's PREMISE WAS FALSE, AND THE REAL DEFECT WAS ONE CONSTANT. The task reads "scrub decodes the EXACT frame (WebCodecs random access) instead of <video> seek granularity", which is a claim nobody here had measured — and O5 is the standing warning about building on one of those. `npm run exp -- f8` (new) seeks BOTH paths to five deliberately off-grid instants and reads the rig's painted timecode off each decoded frame: a <video> element's currentTime and the export's own openVideoChannel().sampleAt() land on the SAME frame every time, 0 ms of rig clock apart, on a VP9 webm from MediaRecorder — and the element is the faster of the two (29 ms mean against 65, the reader's first seek costing 252). So a second decoder in the editor would have bought nothing. What WAS coarse: usePlayback re-seeks a PAUSED element only past 40 ms of drift, and a frame is 33.3 ms, so a scrub could move the playhead a whole frame and leave the picture where it was. PAUSED_SEEK_MS is 15 now (half a frame), A/B-proven on the production build in a real Chrome with a new `ui-shot --flow=scrub`: at 40 the elements move 0 ms on a one-frame ruler step, at 15 they move 32. The rest of F8 — filmstrip thumbnails off the keyframe index, GPU waveforms, the 60 fps-with-a-30-min-take trace — is untouched and still open, now on a premise that has been measured instead of assumed.

td 2026-08-25 — THE INSTANT-SYNC BAND EARNED ITS KEEP WITHIN THE HOUR. A v1 oracle run failed at 93.5 ms maxAbs, and the cause was the other half of "what a composite timestamp means": the file's zero was stamped at recorder.start(), but a canvas capture stream produces a frame when the canvas is PAINTED, so the first sample can be up to a frame later — and stamping a frame early places every copied packet a frame early. DECIDED: v1 dates its file from the first paint at or after start(), with the start() stamp kept as the fallback for a take that stops before painting once. v1 instant path, three cold runs: 44.3 / 48.2 / 40.7 ms against the same takes' render at 57.4 / 49.1 / 34.0 — better than the render on two of three, against 73.9 / 86.3 an hour earlier and 244.8 the day before. LEFT NAMED RATHER THAN CHASED, with numbers: on v2 the instant path is still ~25-33 ms above the same take's render (65.8 / 77.2 / 75.6 vs 41.8 / 51.6 / 42.6) — inside the band, systematically. That engine stamps video frames with their ARRIVAL time while its origin comes from the first audio batch's CAPTURE time, which are two different clocks; that gap is where the next session should start, and not in the export.

td 2026-08-25 (2) — SAFE MODE TOOK THE SOUND TOGGLE OUT OF CHROME'S PICKER. PO, hours after that build shipped: "share sound in chrome with screen toogle fucking not there anymore". Two causes, both ours, both fixed. (1) THE LADDER. The first cut of the wedge recovery dropped `audio` on the FIRST display timeout and kept it dropped for 24h — so Chrome's own "Also share tab audio" checkbox disappeared from the picker for a day, silently, on the strength of a GUESS about which of our four options wedges the browser. That is the frozen rule broken by a fix: nothing that works may be removed. Safe mode is now a ladder ordered by how little the user can see the option go — rung 1 drops size/fps constraints, selfBrowserSurface, surfaceSwitching and the explicit `systemAudio` flag (spec default is 'include', so nothing is lost); rung 2 is the bare `{video:true, audio:true}`; rung 3, and ONLY rung 3, is silent, is reached after three consecutive wedges, and is ONE-SHOT — a success steps back up, so the checkbox returns on the very next take. A rung-0 success still clears the mark, everything still expires 24h after the last wedge, and a stored record from the old cut is read as rung 1, so anyone already carrying one gets audio back on their next click instead of waiting out the TTL. The A/B the old design was reaching for survives — it now costs one take of sound instead of a day, and it isolates the constraints/hints before it ever touches audio. (2) THE PANE. `displaySurface:'monitor'` (2026-08-06, so the default pick records everything rather than one tab that can freeze) opens the picker on Entire Screen — and on macOS/Linux Chromium the tab-audio checkbox exists on the Chrome-Tab pane and NOWHERE else, so a user with Tab Audio lit was being shown a picker that structurally could not offer sound. The hint now follows what the take asked for: Tab Audio on + scope 'tab' opens the Chrome-Tab pane, otherwise the monitor pane is unchanged, and on Windows (scope 'system', monitor shares carry machine audio) nothing moves. Every surface stays pickable either way — displaySurface is a preference, not a restriction. Also: the no-audio-track message stopped telling macOS users to "tick Also share system audio", a box Chrome never shows them; on a monitor/window share with tab-only scope it now says the one thing that would work. Gates: 367 tests (20 new — the rung-by-rung request Chrome actually receives, the legacy-record reading, the pane choice, the message), typecheck clean, verified against the deployed build by intercepting getDisplayMedia and reading the options object with and without a wedge record planted. NOT ANSWERED, unchanged: which option wedges PO's Chrome. The ladder now finds out over four takes instead of asserting it on take one.

td 2026-08-25 (3) — THE SOUND CHECKBOX IS NOW STRUCTURAL, NOT A PREFERENCE. Third report in one day, the last one after a plain reload ("i realoded chrome and sound checkboxes not there again"), which is the datapoint that killed the previous two theories: it is not our localStorage (a reload does not set the wedge mark) and not the request (the deployed build was verifiably sending `audio`). WHAT IT IS: `displaySurface` only chooses which pane Chrome's picker OPENS on, and Chrome overrides that with its own memory of the last surface the site shared. Share Entire Screen once and every later picker opens on Entire Screen — and on macOS/Linux Chromium the "Also share tab audio" checkbox exists ONLY on the Chrome-Tab pane, because a monitor or window share there is mute by construction. So the box was gone permanently, across reloads, and no hint could win it back. FIX: `monitorTypeSurfaces: 'exclude'` (Chromium 112+) while Tab Audio is lit — the Entire-Screen pane is removed from the picker, so there is nothing for Chrome to remember and nowhere to land without sound. It rides EVERY rung of the wedge ladder because it belongs to the user's ask, not to our options. COST, stated rather than hidden: with Tab Audio on you cannot pick a whole screen. That combination has never recorded sound on this OS — it produced a silent channel and an apology — so what is removed is an impossible choice, not a working one; the chip is one click from restoring the old picker, and Windows (scope 'system', monitor shares carry machine audio) is untouched. SECOND HALF OF THE SAME DAY'S RULE ("i need this shit never happen to user, always fucking clean"): the wedge ladder lost its silent rung entirely. It now bottoms out at `{video:true, audio:true}` and there must never be a rung below it — safe mode may drop OUR options, never one the user chose, at any depth, and acquire.test.ts pins that invariant with the note that deleting the test is itself the bug. Also: every take now prints its exact getDisplayMedia options to the console (three rounds were spent arguing about what we send), and the editor's "missing from this take" banner stopped telling macOS users to tick "Also share system audio", a box Chrome never shows them. Gates: 372 tests (22 new), typecheck clean, verified on the deployed build — sound on → monitorTypeSurfaces exclude + tab pane; sound off → the 2026-08-06 monitor picker, byte for byte. STILL OPEN: the wedge itself, and whether Chrome's surface memory is per-origin or per-profile (the fix does not depend on the answer).

po 2026-08-25 — RULE, FROM THE PO, AFTER THIS SESSION BROKE IT: "never change anything about app behaviour without permission". Not a preference and not scoped to big changes. Today's session, chasing a missing sound checkbox, (a) moved which pane Chrome's picker opens on and (b) removed the Entire-Screen option from the picker entirely while Tab Audio was on — neither was asked for, both were shipped inside a bug fix, and (b) took away a way of recording the PO uses. It also rewrote two user-facing messages on the same unverified theory. ALL FOUR REVERTED (commit below): the getDisplayMedia options are byte-for-byte the 2026-08-06 request again — monitor pane hint, every surface offered, "Also share system audio" wording restored. THE FACT THAT KILLED THE THEORY, and it is the PO's, from his own screen: a WHOLE-SCREEN share in his Chrome DOES have a share-sound toggle. So `displayAudioScopeFor(chromium, macos) = 'tab'` in capabilities.ts is probably wrong on current Chrome/macOS, and with it the "Tab Audio" chip label and the tab-shares-only copy. NOT CHANGED — that is a behaviour change and it needs PO's word plus a look at a real picker. WHAT SURVIVES from today, because it is the bug he reported and confirmed fixed: safe mode can never drop a channel the user switched on (no silent rung at any depth, pinned by a test), a stale wedge record from this morning's build reads as the harmless rung, and every take prints its exact getDisplayMedia options to the console. THE ONE-LINE LESSON: a fix may repair what is broken; it may not redesign what is not, and "I was sure it could not work anyway" is not evidence — the user's machine is.

td 2026-08-25 (4) — "GO ROADMAP, ALL O AND F IN ONE", ELEVENTH SESSION: ONE FEATURE, ONE P1, AND TWO IDEAS KILLED WITH NUMBERS. "All O and F" was again not achievable in one session, and the two things that cost the most were the two that shipped no feature — which is the correct trade, but it has to be said out loud rather than buried. (1) F8's FILMSTRIP, the half of F8 that survived yesterday's refutation of its premise: every video lane in the timeline now shows the take's own frames. Two decisions are the whole design. RANDOM ACCESS, not compose/video.ts's forward walk — that reader decodes every frame between the instants it is asked for, which is right for a render and would decode 54,000 frames to draw a strip over a 30-minute take; mediabunny's samplesAtTimestamps seeks to the keyframe before each instant instead. And ONE STITCHED IMAGE, not N elements, because F8's remaining gate is 60 fps with a 30-minute take loaded and a thousand <img> nodes is the way to fail it. The layout rule is a PITCH in CSS px rather than a count, so take length changes what a thumbnail STANDS FOR and never how many there are, and MAX_THUMBS = 24 is a decode budget wearing a layout label. Measured on the prod build in real Chrome: screen 11/11 frames in 493-958 ms, camera 11/11 in 291-315 ms — about 45-85 ms per thumbnail against F8's own measured 65 ms per random-access decode. The screenshot also caught a regression the code could not: .tl is a fixed 184 px and its lane list already overflowed at four channels, so two lanes growing 10 px each pushed Mic off the bottom — the lane is 30 px and the gap tightens to 5 px when any strip exists, which lands the block within 3 px of where it was. (2) F7d, and its re-anchor branch is REFUSED ON MEASUREMENT. A fresh o11 run scores F7's fallback model against the real rendered file at +86.4 / +30.5 / −4.5 / −22.2 / −21.0 % on screen content and −68.4 / −77.1 / −79.0 / −75.6 / −75.3 % on motion: THE ERROR FLIPS SIGN WITH CONTENT, which is the same tell that killed probe attempts 1 and 4, and no single correction survives a sign flip. The mechanism is in the same report and it is not a fudge factor — the composite is 0.24x the render's bytes on motion and 0.78x on screen, because the capture encoder is REALTIME and must keep up while the render's is not, a 3.2x spread from one anchor. So the fallback says what it is instead. The defect that made this urgent was never the number: `measured` stayed null forever on any take the probe cannot run, so "Measuring the other sizes on this take — they settle in a few seconds" sat on screen permanently over numbers nothing was going to settle. The probe now reports running/measured/unavailable and the third state gets its own sentence, styled the OPPOSITE way to the first (a number about to improve recedes; a number that can be several times out does not). Found while proving it on a real build, and worse than the task named: a take with NO COMPOSITE called its default step "Instant" and said "that size is the file, not a guess" while showing the 8 Mbps ceiling — 5.6 MB claimed as the file for an audio-only take of a few hundred KB — when compose/choose.ts cannot packet-copy without a composite and that export fully renders. The panel now asks the same question the export ladder asks. (3) O4-POLISH'S SYNC ANCHOR IS RETIRED, built and measured and deleted, the same verdict shape O5 reached about pipelining the export. P0-instant-sync left the idea that video frames should be stamped with their own capture time instead of their arrival time. `npm run exp -- o4clock` (new) says no three times over: the frame timestamp's epoch is TAKE-RELATIVE (first frame reads 0), so a min-filter's zero is the first frame's ARRIVAL — the very latency it was meant to remove, and the browser exposes no capture-time reference for a local video track the way it does for audio; the jitter actually available to remove is 2.7-3.0 ms (4.1 at p95) against an instrument noise floor of 8-9 ms 1σ; and the media clock runs −20 to −26 ppm, so adopting it trades a 3 ms fixed error for 18-23 ms of DRIFT across a 900 s take, which is the length PO records. The residual is provably not jitter either: the oracle rig uses the same canvas sources, where jitter is 1 ms sd, and still reads the instant path 25-33 ms above the render. What would close it is named for the next session — the composite and the raw channels need one shared media clock, which arrives with O3b, not here. (4) FOUND, NOT FIXED, AND NOW FIRST ON THE MAP: the cold oracle is flaky and it is not this session's code — `--cold=3` fails 2 of 6 runs on a clean build of HEAD, and both failures land on multiples of the rig's own 1 s fiducial grid (one reads −465 ms on every flash pair with maxAbs == |mean| exactly, another reads the render inside the band at 82.7 while the packet-copy paths read −909). The beeps are a uniform 1 s grid, so click↔flash pairing is ambiguous by construction and fiducial.ts snaps to it behind an intervalMs/2 guard — exactly where 465 and 909 come from — and the existing `aliased` flag does not cover this case and read false on every failing run. It is P0 because the same snapping can hide a real error as easily as invent one, and the fast paths are what it hits. Task GATE-alias carries the reproduction, the one-sample bisect (8c73633 clean, HEAD 4/6) and the shape of a fix (make the pairing unique — the video side already carries a per-frame barcode, the audio side does not); it deliberately carries no code. (5) NOTE 10 GOT A FOURTH INSTANCE AND A SCRIPT: 25 minutes went into a rig that had silently died because editing this worktree reloads the harness page through HMR — the node process then sits at 0 % CPU until its timeout with the log frozen on "ephemeral server on …". STATE has said "run long cells from a /tmp mirror" since yesterday; `sh scripts/mirror-measure.sh <npm script>` is now that sentence as a command, and it mirrors the WORKING TREE so uncommitted changes are actually under test. GATES: 399 tests (16 new), typecheck clean, warm oracle PASS on v2 and on v1, fidelity PASS (toneErr 0.03 dB, separation 59.2, 0 limiter hits), browser-check real Chrome 151 8/8 with zero console errors, first paint 241,803 B — byte-identical to a build of HEAD, so this session added nothing to it.

td 2026-08-25 (5) — GATE-alias: THE COLD ORACLE WAS MEASURING PAST THE END OF ITS OWN RULER, AND THE FIRST DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG. The gate failed about one cold run in five with a whole file scored a few hundred milliseconds out of sync — reproduced on a clean `git archive HEAD` build, so not this session's code. The entry I wrote when I found it said the click↔flash PAIRING was ambiguous on a uniform 1 s grid and the estimator was picking the wrong alignment. That sounded right, it was consistent with every symptom, and it was not the cause. What settled it was refusing to keep arguing from the summary line: a failing run in a `--cold=N` matrix used to print its failure strings and throw the report away, so three sessions of cold flakes had been theorised about from one line of output. It now dumps the whole report to /tmp, and the first captured failure answered the question in a minute. THE NUMBERS: beep arrivals 1537, 2537, 3537, 4537, 5537 — a residual of 537 ms against the nominal k·1000 grid on EVERY one, while the flash side stalled 19-20. The AudioContext startup stall was 537 ms, perfectly regular and trivially measurable, and it was past TWO hard limits at once: estimateScheduleSkewFromArrivals refused any stall over MAX_SCHEDULE_STALL_MS = 450, so there was no correction at all and the gate fell to its RAW rung (which is why the failure line said `(raw)` and why "aliasing" was the wrong story); and flashSyncStats's pairing window is half the grid wide and centred on ZERO, so the +562 ms offset that stall produces is not representable and came back as its complement against the next flash, −438. The run was in sync. The ruler was too short and nothing said so. FOUR FIXES, each measured. (1) THE STALL IS ARITHMETIC, NOT A SEARCH: a beep is SCHEDULED at k·interval and can only render LATE — an AudioContext that has not finished starting cannot make a sound early — so arrival = k·interval + stall with stall in [0, interval) and k = floor(arrival/interval). The candidate-index sweep and its clamp are gone; a missed first beep still works, because that moves k and not the relation. The magnitude clamps inside isAliasedScheduleCorrection went with them, because what distinguishes an alias from a stall is not SIZE but whether the raw reading agrees — a real stall is visible in the raw offset too, and that clause is the one that caught every historical flake, so it stays. (2) THE PAIRING IS TOLD WHERE TO LOOK: the rig measures both reference schedules before it decodes anything and their difference is where a correctly-paired export must land, so the window is centred there instead of on zero; omitted, it is the old behaviour exactly. (3) THE PAIRING PICKS BY CONSENSUS, NOT BY PAIR COUNT — the old sweep kept whichever alignment produced the most pairs, which is injection-proven wrong: with a doubled detector it reports −400 ms where the truth is +50. Now every (onset, flash) pair proposes an offset and the one the most events independently agree on wins, ties to the smaller |offset|, and that prior is argued rather than convenient (the band is ±90 ms against a 1000 ms grid, so "a whole second out" needs more evidence than "in band", not less). (4) THE RIG WARMS ITS ENCODER BECAUSE PRODUCTION DOES — note 6's fourth instance. A Chrome PROCESS's first VideoEncoder pays a multi-second init and every oracle run is a fresh process; production pays it at MOUNT (prearm.ts → encoderWarm.ts) while this rig paid it DURING the take, which on a 6 s take is the whole take. Two dumped failures show it plainly: the render found all five flashes while the packet-copied exports found one and none. TWO VACUITY HOLES CLOSED WITH IT, and the second caught a real run within minutes of being written: the sync bands were guarded by `typeof mean === 'number'`, so a fast path that RAN but produced no number pushed no failure and the run passed green on a measurement never taken. Both the instant and the trimmed path now fail in that case. ONE SAFETY NET for what the arithmetic still cannot see: a stall of a FULL interval wraps silently (1100 ms reads as 100 with total confidence), nothing in the signal can distinguish it, so the gate refuses a run whose stall exceeds three quarters of the grid — measured headroom ~130 ms before the rig warmed its encoder and 326-531 ms after, so that is a real margin and not a hair trigger. MEASURED AFTER, 22 cold runs across three batches: zero sync failures, zero aliasing, zero `n/a` fast-path numbers, and EVERY run on the `metric=symmetric` rung where before raw, corrected and n/a were scattered across runs; sync 36.6-65.4 ms, instant 41.8-78.5, smart cut 41.3-78.8. TRIED AND DELETED, in the same spirit as O5's lookahead: a 750 ms settle between the warm and the take, on the theory that they were competing for the machine — eight cold runs with it (stall 326-498) against eight without (343-462) say they are not, so it went rather than being kept as a comforting no-op. The warm does raise the stall about threefold and that is simply its price. STILL OPEN AND NOT THIS: the SPUR band fails about one cold run in seven at −33 to −38 dB against −40, on a settled machine (1 of 6) as well as a busy one (2 of 8). That is the capture-starvation artifact STATE already describes for the fidelity oracle, it predates this work, and it is the only remaining cold-gate flake. THE LESSON, and it is not the one I expected to write: an instrument that cannot report its own limits will report a defect instead. Every symptom here pointed at the product or at the pairing, and the truth was a constant in a file nobody had looked at in months — visible immediately once the rig was made to hand over its raw numbers on the run that failed, rather than on a rerun that might not.
